window.QEVA_LEARNING={"schema_version":"qeva-learning-bundle/1","release":"0.4.0","concepts":[{"schema_version":"qeva-learning-concept/1","id":"qeva:learning:chaos","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:1:chaos@1","title":"Chaos","difficulty":"advanced","prerequisites":[{"ref":"qeva:learning:sensitivity@1","minimum_state":"understood","reason":"Understand finite separation versus the quantified sensitivity property."}],"related":["qeva:1:sensitivity@1","qeva:1:nonlinearity@1"],"explanation":{"intuition":"A surprising picture is the beginning of a question, not the end of an argument.","manipulation":"Label the evidence from a run, then identify what a precise claim would have to declare.","pattern":"Random-looking behavior, sensitivity, and a named chaos criterion are not interchangeable labels.","mechanism":"QEVA’s seed record treats chaos as a family of contextual notions. A claim needs a specified space, map, criterion, hypotheses, and supporting argument.","vocabulary":[{"term":"criterion","meaning":"The exact property a claim asserts."},{"term":"conjecture","meaning":"A stated proposition whose required proof is not supplied here."}],"notation":[{"symbol":"Chaos(T)","meaning":"Not a universal predicate assigned by this archive; its meaning must be declared."}],"formal":{"source_ref":"qeva:1:chaos@1","text":"QEVA Protocol 0.1 assigns no universal predicate Chaos(T). A record claiming chaos must identify a contextual criterion (for example a named topological, measure-theoretic, symbolic, or metric definition) and state its hypotheses.","context":"No single formal framework is asserted for this context-dependent seed record.","caveat":"Popular descriptions such as “random-looking” or “small errors grow” are not by themselves mathematical definitions of chaos."},"proof":{"label":"scope-boundary","statement":"This route does not prove a general theorem about chaos or the logistic map.","steps":[],"limitation":"The terminal task is claim formulation and evidence literacy. A named chaos definition and its theorem-specific prerequisites belong in a separately reviewed route."},"frontier":"Formulate a precise candidate, make dependencies explicit, and seek review. Do not promote this local learning result to archive verification."},"activities":["qeva:activity:chaos-label@1","qeva:activity:chaos-frontier@1"],"mastery":{"understood":["qeva:activity:chaos-label@1"],"mastered":["qeva:activity:chaos-frontier@1"]},"provenance":{"kind":"editorial-learning-overlay","source_refs":["qeva:1:chaos@1"],"created":"2026-09-09","license":"CC0-1.0","review_status":"draft-for-mathematical-review"}},{"schema_version":"qeva-learning-concept/1","id":"qeva:learning:collatz-conjecture","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:1:collatz-conjecture@1","title":"Collatz conjecture","difficulty":"advanced","prerequisites":[{"ref":"qeva:learning:collatz-map@1","minimum_state":"understood","reason":"State the exact map before a claim about all its orbits."},{"ref":"qeva:learning:periodicity@1","minimum_state":"understood","reason":"Distinguish reaching a particular cycle from universal eventual behavior."}],"related":["qeva:1:collatz-map@1","qeva:1:hierarchy@1"],"explanation":{"intuition":"A small rule can generate a question much larger than any finite run.","manipulation":"Compare a completed orbit with a run that stops at its budget. Retain both in a notebook.","pattern":"Successful tested cases and unfinished runs do not settle a claim quantified over every positive integer.","mechanism":"The pinned seed archive classifies the universal claim as a conjecture. This learning layer neither changes that record nor asserts a resolution.","vocabulary":[{"term":"universal claim","meaning":"A proposition about every member of a declared domain."},{"term":"counterexample","meaning":"A case that actually refutes the stated claim; a time limit alone does not."}],"notation":[{"symbol":"∀n > 0, ∃k ≥ 0 : Tᵏ(n) = 1","meaning":"For every positive starting integer there is a finite number of updates reaching 1."}],"formal":{"source_ref":"qeva:1:collatz-conjecture@1","text":"For every n∈N_{>0}, there exists k≥0 such that T^k(n)=1.","context":"positive integers","caveat":"This is open. Error-amplification, chaos, hierarchy, or emergent-invariance descriptions are research hypotheses unless made precise and proved."},"proof":{"label":"scope-boundary","statement":"No proof or refutation of the universal conjecture is supplied here.","steps":[],"limitation":"Finite computations can establish finite orbit facts. Running out of iterations does not prove that an orbit never reaches 1."},"frontier":"A personal lab can become a reviewable candidate. Review and explicit verification, not team approval counts, control entry into a QEVA release."},"activities":["qeva:activity:collatz-evidence@1","qeva:activity:collatz-review@1"],"mastery":{"understood":["qeva:activity:collatz-evidence@1"],"mastered":["qeva:activity:collatz-review@1"]},"provenance":{"kind":"editorial-learning-overlay","source_refs":["qeva:1:collatz-conjecture@1"],"created":"2026-09-09","license":"CC0-1.0","review_status":"draft-for-mathematical-review"}},{"schema_version":"qeva-learning-concept/1","id":"qeva:learning:collatz-map","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:1:collatz-map@1","title":"Collatz map","difficulty":"developing","prerequisites":[{"ref":"qeva:learning:recurrence@1","minimum_state":"understood","reason":"An orbit is generated by repeatedly applying the update."},{"ref":"qeva:learning:parity@1","minimum_state":"understood","reason":"Parity selects the even or odd branch."}],"related":["qeva:1:periodicity@1","qeva:1:collatz-conjecture@1"],"explanation":{"intuition":"One machine, two branches: halve an even integer, or triple an odd integer and add one.","manipulation":"Choose a positive start and an iteration budget. Use exact integer arithmetic and retain the stopping reason.","pattern":"Some steps rise and others fall. The orbit from 6 reaches 1 after eight updates.","mechanism":"The state is a positive integer. Its parity uniquely selects one of two arithmetic updates. A stopping budget belongs to the computation, not the mathematical map.","vocabulary":[{"term":"piecewise rule","meaning":"A rule whose branch depends on a stated condition."},{"term":"iteration budget","meaning":"A bound on the work a run is allowed to perform."}],"notation":[{"symbol":"T(n)","meaning":"One Collatz update, not the entire orbit."},{"symbol":"Tᵏ(n)","meaning":"k successive applications of T."}],"formal":{"source_ref":"qeva:1:collatz-map@1","text":"T(n)=n/2 if n is even, and T(n)=3n+1 if n is odd, for n∈N_{>0}. The orbit is n,T(n),T^2(n),....","context":"positive integers","caveat":"The map is deterministic and discrete, but calling it chaotic requires a separate precise definition and proof."},"proof":{"label":"worked-example","statement":"The odd branch sends an odd integer to an even integer.","steps":["Write an odd input as n = 2k + 1 with integer k.","Compute 3n + 1 = 6k + 4 = 2(3k + 2).","The witness 3k + 2 is an integer, so the output is even."],"limitation":"This local parity fact does not prove that every positive orbit reaches 1."},"frontier":"Separate a checked orbit, an eventual cycle, and a universal conjecture."},"activities":["qeva:activity:collatz-step@1","qeva:activity:collatz-run@1","qeva:activity:collatz-even-proof@1"],"mastery":{"understood":["qeva:activity:collatz-step@1","qeva:activity:collatz-run@1"],"mastered":["qeva:activity:collatz-even-proof@1"]},"provenance":{"kind":"editorial-learning-overlay","source_refs":["qeva:1:collatz-map@1"],"created":"2026-09-09","license":"CC0-1.0","review_status":"draft-for-mathematical-review"}},{"schema_version":"qeva-learning-concept/1","id":"qeva:learning:fixed-point","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:1:fixed-point@1","title":"Fixed point","difficulty":"developing","prerequisites":[{"ref":"qeva:learning:recurrence@1","minimum_state":"understood","reason":"Iteration gives the context for a state that reproduces itself."}],"related":["qeva:1:stability@1","qeva:1:periodicity@1"],"explanation":{"intuition":"Some states go through the machine without changing.","manipulation":"Find a value whose output equals its input. Do not confuse it with a nearby state.","pattern":"For x ↦ x², both 0 and 1 stay fixed. Their surrounding behavior need not be the same.","mechanism":"The fixed-point equation F(x) = x converts a dynamical question into an equation. Stability requires additional analysis of neighbors.","vocabulary":[{"term":"fixed point","meaning":"A state left unchanged by one application of a map."}],"notation":[{"symbol":"F(x*) = x*","meaning":"The starred state is unchanged; the star is a label, not multiplication."}],"formal":{"source_ref":"qeva:1:fixed-point@1","text":"x*∈X is a fixed point of F iff F(x*)=x*.","context":"set/function theory","caveat":"A fixed point may be stable, unstable, attracting, repelling, or neutral depending on additional structure and the chosen stability notion."},"proof":{"label":"worked-example","statement":"The real fixed points of F(x) = x² are exactly 0 and 1.","steps":["A fixed point satisfies x² = x.","Rearrangement gives x(x − 1) = 0.","The real zero-product property gives x = 0 or x = 1.","Substitution verifies both possibilities."],"limitation":"This algebraic worked example is not machine-checked. It says nothing by itself about stability."},"frontier":"What happens to a state just beside a fixed point?"},"activities":["qeva:activity:fixed-point-find@1","qeva:activity:fixed-point-proof@1"],"mastery":{"understood":["qeva:activity:fixed-point-find@1"],"mastered":["qeva:activity:fixed-point-proof@1"]},"provenance":{"kind":"editorial-learning-overlay","source_refs":["qeva:1:fixed-point@1"],"created":"2026-09-09","license":"CC0-1.0","review_status":"draft-for-mathematical-review"}},{"schema_version":"qeva-learning-concept/1","id":"qeva:learning:nonlinearity","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:1:nonlinearity@1","title":"Nonlinearity","difficulty":"developing","prerequisites":[{"ref":"qeva:learning:recurrence@1","minimum_state":"understood","reason":"This route studies input-output rules before their iterated behavior."}],"related":["qeva:1:sensitivity@1","qeva:1:optimization@1"],"explanation":{"intuition":"Combining the inputs need not combine their effects.","manipulation":"Calculate f(1 + 1) and f(1) + f(1) separately for f(x) = x².","pattern":"The two outputs differ: 4 is not 2. Superposition fails in this example.","mechanism":"Linearity is a universal requirement across inputs and scalars. One valid counterexample is enough to establish failure of that requirement.","vocabulary":[{"term":"superposition","meaning":"Compatibility with adding inputs and multiplying them by scalars."},{"term":"nonlinear","meaning":"Not satisfying the stated linearity condition."}],"notation":[{"symbol":"f(ax + by) = af(x) + bf(y)","meaning":"The linearity requirement for every permitted choice."}],"formal":{"source_ref":"qeva:1:nonlinearity@1","text":"A map f:V→W is linear iff f(ax+by)=af(x)+bf(y) for all x,y and scalars a,b. It is nonlinear if this condition fails.","context":"linear algebra","caveat":"Nonlinearity alone does not imply complexity, instability, or chaos."},"proof":{"label":"worked-example","statement":"The map f(x) = x² from ℝ to ℝ is nonlinear.","steps":["Take inputs x = y = 1 and scalars a = b = 1.","Then f(x + y) = 4, but f(x) + f(y) = 2.","Since 4 ≠ 2, the universal linearity condition fails."],"limitation":"This counterexample does not imply instability, complexity, or chaos."},"frontier":"Which parameter choices change an iterated nonlinear rule’s behavior?"},"activities":["qeva:activity:nonlinearity-counterexample@1","qeva:activity:nonlinearity-scope@1"],"mastery":{"understood":["qeva:activity:nonlinearity-counterexample@1"],"mastered":["qeva:activity:nonlinearity-scope@1"]},"provenance":{"kind":"editorial-learning-overlay","source_refs":["qeva:1:nonlinearity@1"],"created":"2026-09-09","license":"CC0-1.0","review_status":"draft-for-mathematical-review"}},{"schema_version":"qeva-learning-concept/1","id":"qeva:learning:parity","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:1:parity@1","title":"Parity","difficulty":"entry","prerequisites":[],"related":["qeva:1:collatz-map@1","qeva:1:equivalence@1"],"explanation":{"intuition":"Group integers according to whether they can be made entirely of pairs.","manipulation":"Supply an integer witness for an odd value: n = 2k + 1.","pattern":"Every integer belongs to the even or odd class under ordinary integer arithmetic.","mechanism":"The representation n = 2k or n = 2k + 1 makes a classification checkable and can choose a branch of an update rule.","vocabulary":[{"term":"even","meaning":"An integer of the form 2k for integer k."},{"term":"odd","meaning":"An integer of the form 2k + 1 for integer k."}],"notation":[{"symbol":"n = 2k + 1","meaning":"An integer witness k exhibits oddness."}],"formal":{"source_ref":"qeva:1:parity@1","text":"For n in Z: n is even iff there exists k in Z with n=2k. n is odd iff there exists k in Z with n=2k+1.","context":"integers with ordinary arithmetic","caveat":null},"proof":{"label":"worked-example","statement":"27 is odd.","steps":["Choose the integer k = 13.","Then 2k + 1 = 2 × 13 + 1 = 27."],"limitation":"This exhibits oddness for one integer; it is not a general result about Collatz orbits."},"frontier":"What happens when parity decides which update rule to apply?"},"activities":["qeva:activity:parity-sort@1","qeva:activity:parity-witness@1"],"mastery":{"understood":["qeva:activity:parity-sort@1"],"mastered":["qeva:activity:parity-witness@1"]},"provenance":{"kind":"editorial-learning-overlay","source_refs":["qeva:1:parity@1"],"created":"2026-09-09","license":"CC0-1.0","review_status":"draft-for-mathematical-review"}},{"schema_version":"qeva-learning-concept/1","id":"qeva:learning:periodicity","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:1:periodicity@1","title":"Periodicity of a sequence","difficulty":"developing","prerequisites":[{"ref":"qeva:learning:recurrence@1","minimum_state":"understood","reason":"Use a declared update or repetition rule, not a guessed finite pattern."}],"related":["qeva:1:collatz-conjecture@1","qeva:1:quasiperiodicity@1"],"explanation":{"intuition":"An entire pattern can return after a fixed number of steps.","manipulation":"Follow a declared repeating block and distinguish it from any transient prefix.","pattern":"The cycle 1 → 4 → 2 → 1 repeats every three updates under the Collatz rule.","mechanism":"Periodicity is an equality across all permitted shifted indices. Eventual periodicity only requires repetition after some finite prefix.","vocabulary":[{"term":"period","meaning":"A positive index shift preserving every term in the stated domain."},{"term":"transient","meaning":"A prefix before a repeating part begins."}],"notation":[{"symbol":"a[n + p] = a[n]","meaning":"The same value appears p steps later for every stated index."}],"formal":{"source_ref":"qeva:1:periodicity@1","text":"A sequence (a_n) is periodic with period p>0 iff a_{n+p}=a_n for every index n for which both terms are in the domain. A least positive such p, when it exists, is called the fundamental period.","context":"sequences indexed by integers or natural numbers","caveat":"Continuous functions, group actions, tilings, and dynamical systems use related but context-specific notions of periodicity."},"proof":{"label":"worked-example","statement":"Under T, the states 1, 4, 2 form a three-cycle.","steps":["The odd branch gives T(1) = 4.","The even branch gives T(4) = 2 and T(2) = 1.","The deterministic update returns to the initial state after three steps; the three states are distinct."],"limitation":"This establishes a particular cycle. It does not show that all starting states enter it."},"frontier":"Does every positive integer reach this cycle? That is a different quantified statement."},"activities":["qeva:activity:periodicity-block@1","qeva:activity:periodicity-prefix@1"],"mastery":{"understood":["qeva:activity:periodicity-block@1"],"mastered":["qeva:activity:periodicity-prefix@1"]},"provenance":{"kind":"editorial-learning-overlay","source_refs":["qeva:1:periodicity@1"],"created":"2026-09-09","license":"CC0-1.0","review_status":"draft-for-mathematical-review"}},{"schema_version":"qeva-learning-concept/1","id":"qeva:learning:recurrence","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:1:recurrence@1","title":"Recurrence relation","difficulty":"entry","prerequisites":[{"ref":"qeva:learning:sequence@1","minimum_state":"understood","reason":"A recurrence relates indexed values of a sequence."}],"related":["qeva:1:fixed-point@1","qeva:1:sensitivity@1","qeva:1:closed-form@1"],"explanation":{"intuition":"Build a machine whose output can become its next input.","manipulation":"Change the initial value while keeping the update rule fixed. Compare the resulting sequences.","pattern":"The same machine can produce different orbits from different beginnings.","mechanism":"An explicit single-valued update and a starting state determine successive states. More general recurrence relations can need additional data or uniqueness conditions.","vocabulary":[{"term":"recurrence","meaning":"A relation constraining values across index steps."},{"term":"initial condition","meaning":"The stated value or values from which an update begins."},{"term":"orbit","meaning":"The successive states obtained by iterating a map."}],"notation":[{"symbol":"x[n+1] = F(x[n])","meaning":"Apply F to the current value to obtain the next value."}],"formal":{"source_ref":"qeva:1:recurrence@1","text":"A recurrence relation for a sequence (a_n) is a relation R(n,a_n,a_{n-1},...,a_{n-k}) that is required to hold for specified n. A recurrence plus initial conditions may determine the sequence.","context":"standard discrete mathematics","caveat":null},"proof":{"label":"worked-example","statement":"For x[n+1] = 2x[n] and x[0] = c, x[n] = 2ⁿc for all n ≥ 0.","steps":["At n = 0, 2⁰c = c, so the initial condition holds.","Assume x[n] = 2ⁿc. The rule gives x[n+1] = 2 × 2ⁿc = 2ⁿ⁺¹c.","Induction establishes the formula for every nonnegative integer n."],"limitation":"Ordinary real arithmetic and induction are external framework assumptions, not fully expanded archive dependencies."},"frontier":"Can a state reproduce itself? Can nearby states behave differently?"},"activities":["qeva:activity:recurrence-step@1","qeva:activity:recurrence-build@1","qeva:activity:recurrence-unique@1"],"mastery":{"understood":["qeva:activity:recurrence-step@1","qeva:activity:recurrence-build@1"],"mastered":["qeva:activity:recurrence-unique@1"]},"provenance":{"kind":"editorial-learning-overlay","source_refs":["qeva:1:recurrence@1"],"created":"2026-09-09","license":"CC0-1.0","review_status":"draft-for-mathematical-review"}},{"schema_version":"qeva-learning-concept/1","id":"qeva:learning:sensitivity","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:1:sensitivity@1","title":"Sensitive dependence on initial conditions","difficulty":"developing","prerequisites":[{"ref":"qeva:learning:stability@1","minimum_state":"understood","reason":"First distinguish a reference state from a neighborhood of states."},{"ref":"qeva:learning:nonlinearity@1","minimum_state":"understood","reason":"Separate structural nonlinearity from claims about error amplification."}],"related":["qeva:1:chaos@1","qeva:1:recurrence@1"],"explanation":{"intuition":"Two nearly identical beginnings can become visibly different. The word “can” needs careful quantifiers.","manipulation":"Run paired logistic trajectories and change the feedback parameter. Keep the initial separation and stopping time explicit.","pattern":"Different parameters can change the observed separation pattern. A finite pair explores particular states, not every neighborhood.","mechanism":"The archive’s definition requires a uniform positive threshold, every base point and radius, and some nearby witness and time. Binary64 rounding can also affect numerical trajectories.","vocabulary":[{"term":"sensitive dependence","meaning":"The particular quantified metric property quoted below."},{"term":"metric","meaning":"The declared way of measuring distance."}],"notation":[{"symbol":"d(x, y)","meaning":"Distance between two states in the chosen metric."},{"symbol":"Tⁿ(x)","meaning":"Apply the map T n times to x."}],"formal":{"source_ref":"qeva:1:sensitivity@1","text":"T has sensitive dependence on initial conditions iff there exists δ>0 such that for every x in X and every ε>0 there exist y in X and n>=0 with d(x,y)\u003cε and d(T^n(x),T^n(y))>δ.","context":"metric dynamical systems","caveat":"Sensitivity alone is not a universal definition of chaos."},"proof":{"label":"scope-boundary","statement":"These numerical runs do not prove the archive’s universal sensitivity statement.","steps":[],"limitation":"No sensitivity theorem for the displayed logistic parameters is claimed. The experiment addresses only its finite trajectories."},"frontier":"Which exact definition is intended when someone calls this chaos?"},"activities":["qeva:activity:sensitivity-compare@1","qeva:activity:sensitivity-quantifiers@1"],"mastery":{"understood":["qeva:activity:sensitivity-compare@1"],"mastered":["qeva:activity:sensitivity-quantifiers@1"]},"provenance":{"kind":"editorial-learning-overlay","source_refs":["qeva:1:sensitivity@1"],"created":"2026-09-09","license":"CC0-1.0","review_status":"draft-for-mathematical-review"}},{"schema_version":"qeva-learning-concept/1","id":"qeva:learning:sequence","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:1:sequence@1","title":"Sequence","difficulty":"entry","prerequisites":[],"related":["qeva:1:recurrence@1","qeva:1:periodicity@1"],"explanation":{"intuition":"Before symbols, there is a position and something at that position. Move one place along a declared rule.","manipulation":"Predict the next value, then pair the displayed values with the indices 0, 1, 2, 3.","pattern":"Doubling from 1 gives 1, 2, 4, 8, 16. This is one example, not the definition of every sequence.","mechanism":"The index is an input. The sequence assigns it one value. A finite prefix alone cannot uniquely determine an unstated infinite rule.","vocabulary":[{"term":"index","meaning":"The input specifying a position."},{"term":"term","meaning":"The value assigned to one index."}],"notation":[{"symbol":"aₙ","meaning":"The value assigned to index n."},{"symbol":"a : I → X","meaning":"A function from the declared index set to a value space."}],"formal":{"source_ref":"qeva:1:sequence@1","text":"A sequence in X indexed by I is a function a:I→X. When I=N, the value at n is written a_n.","context":"standard functions","caveat":null},"proof":{"label":"worked-example","statement":"For the declared rule aₙ = 2ⁿ, aₙ₊₁ = 2aₙ.","steps":["For n ≥ 0, exponent laws give 2ⁿ⁺¹ = 2 × 2ⁿ.","Substitute aₙ = 2ⁿ to obtain aₙ₊₁ = 2aₙ."],"limitation":"This checks this example; it does not infer a unique law from an unexplained list."},"frontier":"How much initial information is needed when a rule relates neighboring terms?"},"activities":["qeva:activity:sequence-next@1","qeva:activity:sequence-index@1"],"mastery":{"understood":["qeva:activity:sequence-next@1"],"mastered":["qeva:activity:sequence-index@1"]},"provenance":{"kind":"editorial-learning-overlay","source_refs":["qeva:1:sequence@1"],"created":"2026-09-09","license":"CC0-1.0","review_status":"draft-for-mathematical-review"}},{"schema_version":"qeva-learning-concept/1","id":"qeva:learning:stability","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:1:stability@1","title":"Stability","difficulty":"developing","prerequisites":[{"ref":"qeva:learning:fixed-point@1","minimum_state":"understood","reason":"Compare perturbations around an identified fixed point."}],"related":["qeva:1:attractor@1","qeva:1:sensitivity@1"],"explanation":{"intuition":"A resting state and a robust resting state are not the same thing.","manipulation":"Perturb zero slightly, then change the multiplier in the update x ↦ ax.","pattern":"Halving contracts the perturbation; doubling amplifies a nonzero perturbation. Both maps fix zero.","mechanism":"Lyapunov stability asks whether an appropriately small initial neighborhood stays inside each requested neighborhood for all future times.","vocabulary":[{"term":"perturbation","meaning":"A specified change in a starting state."},{"term":"Lyapunov stability","meaning":"An all-time neighborhood condition around a reference fixed point."}],"notation":[{"symbol":"ε","meaning":"A requested bound on the distance from the fixed point."},{"symbol":"δ","meaning":"A sufficient bound on the initial distance."}],"formal":{"source_ref":"qeva:1:stability@1","text":"For a fixed point x* of a map F on a metric space, Lyapunov stability means: for every ε>0 there exists δ>0 such that d(x0,x*)\u003cδ implies d(F^n(x0),x*)\u003cε for all n≥0.","context":"metric dynamical systems","caveat":"There are many inequivalent notions of stability: Lyapunov, asymptotic, structural, orbital, input-output, stochastic, and others."},"proof":{"label":"worked-example","statement":"Zero is Lyapunov stable for F(x) = x/2 on ℝ.","steps":["Let ε > 0 and choose δ = ε.","If |x₀| \u003c δ, then the iterated rule gives |Fⁿ(x₀)| = |x₀|/2ⁿ.","For all n ≥ 0 this is at most |x₀|, which is less than ε."],"limitation":"This is one declared stability notion and one map. A finite trajectory is not the all-time argument."},"frontier":"Can a simple rule amplify some nearby starts in every neighborhood?"},"activities":["qeva:activity:stability-compare@1","qeva:activity:stability-proof@1"],"mastery":{"understood":["qeva:activity:stability-compare@1"],"mastered":["qeva:activity:stability-proof@1"]},"provenance":{"kind":"editorial-learning-overlay","source_refs":["qeva:1:stability@1"],"created":"2026-09-09","license":"CC0-1.0","review_status":"draft-for-mathematical-review"}}],"activities":[{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:chaos-frontier","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:chaos@1","type":"choice","stage":"transfer","title":"What would verification require?","prompt":"Before promoting a sandbox claim of chaos into a candidate knowledge object, which package is necessary?","hint":"The archive deliberately does not define one universal Chaos(T) predicate.","config":{"options":[{"id":"0","text":"A compelling animation and many independent shares."},{"id":"1","text":"A declared space and map, a named criterion with its hypotheses, exact dependencies, and an argument addressing that criterion for review."},{"id":"2","text":"A sufficiently large r, because all nonlinear maps with large parameters are chaotic."}],"correct":"1"},"feedback":{"pass":"That is a reviewable claim, not automatic acceptance. This route teaches how to state a chaos claim, not a general theorem proving the logistic map chaotic.","fail":"Name the criterion, its context, and the supporting argument. Popularity and appearance cannot substitute for these."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:chaos@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:chaos-label","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:chaos@1","type":"classification","stage":"verify","title":"Do not upgrade the evidence.","prompt":"Classify the actual support being described, rather than the confidence of its author.","hint":"Read what is supplied: a finite run, a proposed precise claim, or a missing definition.","config":{"labels":[{"id":"observation","text":"Numerical observation"},{"id":"conjecture","text":"Conjecture"},{"id":"underspecified","text":"Underspecified claim"}],"items":[{"id":"0","text":"A pair of computed logistic trajectories separated by more than 0.1 during a declared 70-step run.","label":"observation"},{"id":"1","text":"A researcher proposes that a specified map satisfies a named chaos criterion but supplies no proof.","label":"conjecture"},{"id":"2","text":"The claim “this is chaos” omits the state space, criterion, and hypotheses.","label":"underspecified"}]},"feedback":{"pass":"Labels preserve the distance between seeing behavior, proposing a claim, and proving it.","fail":"A plot is an observation. A precise unsupported proposal is a conjecture. A missing criterion leaves the claim underspecified."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:chaos@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:collatz-even-proof","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:collatz-map@1","type":"choice","stage":"transfer","title":"A local fact that really can be proved.","prompt":"Let n = 2k + 1 be an odd integer. Which proves its next Collatz value is even?","hint":"Substitute the defining form of an odd integer.","config":{"options":[{"id":"0","text":"The graph seems to alternate between odd and even."},{"id":"1","text":"3n + 1 = 3(2k + 1) + 1 = 6k + 4 = 2(3k + 2), and 3k + 2 is an integer."},{"id":"2","text":"The next value is always smaller than n."}],"correct":"1"},"feedback":{"pass":"This proves a particular parity property of the update, not eventual arrival at 1 for every start.","fail":"Use the integer witness in the definition of oddness. The odd branch often increases the value."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:collatz-map@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:collatz-evidence","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:collatz-conjecture@1","type":"classification","stage":"verify","title":"Where does the evidence stop?","prompt":"Label each statement about this experiment or proposed extension.","hint":"A statement about all positive integers is not a statement about one tested start.","config":{"labels":[{"id":"finite","text":"Checked finite orbit"},{"id":"inconclusive","text":"Inconclusive budget stop"},{"id":"universal","text":"Universal conjecture in the seed archive"}],"items":[{"id":"0","text":"An exact integer run from 6 reaches 1 after eight updates.","label":"finite"},{"id":"1","text":"A run from 27 is stopped after two updates without reaching 1.","label":"inconclusive"},{"id":"2","text":"Every positive integer eventually reaches 1 under T.","label":"universal"}]},"feedback":{"pass":"The failed-to-finish run is useful evidence about a budget, not a counterexample. The universal claim remains separate.","fail":"Keep the quantifier “every” separate from a finite computation; preserve the run’s stopping reason."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:collatz-conjecture@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:collatz-review","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:collatz-conjecture@1","type":"choice","stage":"transfer","title":"Can this become a release?","prompt":"A shared notebook contains many successful runs. What should happen next?","hint":"The community pipeline is a review process, not a popularity threshold.","config":{"options":[{"id":"0","text":"Automatically mark the conjecture proved after enough team members approve."},{"id":"1","text":"Preserve the parameters and failures, label the evidence, formulate a candidate claim, and seek explicit mathematical verification before a release."},{"id":"2","text":"Delete runs that did not finish to make the result clearer."}],"correct":"1"},"feedback":{"pass":"Preserve → formulate → review → verify → release. Account state cannot grant mathematical authority.","fail":"Neither popularity nor removing failures changes the scope of a finite experiment."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:collatz-conjecture@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:collatz-run","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:collatz-map@1","type":"experiment","stage":"experiment","title":"Reproduce an orbit.","prompt":"Run the exact integer recurrence. Read the stopping reason, then select the only justified general interpretation.","hint":"Starting at 6 reaches 1 after eight updates. A low budget can stop before reaching 1.","config":{"kernel_ref":"qeva:kernel:collatz@1","parameters":{"start":"6","steps":80},"editable":["start","steps"],"minimum_runs":1,"vary":null,"conclusions":[{"id":"0","text":"A finite run can establish what happened for its declared start within its budget, but not for every positive integer."},{"id":"1","text":"A run that stops at its step budget is a counterexample to the conjecture."},{"id":"2","text":"Every orbit decreases at every step."}],"correct":"0"},"feedback":{"pass":"A finite orbit and a universal statement have different scopes. A budget stop is preserved as inconclusive.","fail":"The stopping budget is a computational limit, not a mathematical refutation."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:collatz-map@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:collatz-step","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:collatz-map@1","type":"number","stage":"predict","title":"Take the correct branch.","prompt":"Under the declared Collatz map, what is T(7)?","hint":"7 is odd, so use 3n + 1, not n/2.","config":{"value":22,"tolerance":0,"display":[]},"feedback":{"pass":"T(7) = 22. A simple piecewise rule can move upward as well as downward.","fail":"Because 7 is odd, compute 3 × 7 + 1."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:collatz-map@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:fixed-point-find","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:fixed-point@1","type":"number","stage":"predict","title":"Find the state that stays.","prompt":"For F(x) = 2x + 1 on the real numbers, which x satisfies F(x) = x?","hint":"Solve 2x + 1 = x. Negative real values are allowed.","config":{"value":-1,"tolerance":0,"display":[]},"feedback":{"pass":"F(−1) = −1. A fixed point is a state left unchanged, not necessarily an attracting state.","fail":"Set the output equal to the input: 2x + 1 = x, so x = −1."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:fixed-point@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:fixed-point-proof","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:fixed-point@1","type":"order","stage":"verify","title":"Make the reasoning explicit.","prompt":"Arrange this complete elementary argument for all fixed points of F(x) = x² on ℝ.","hint":"Start from the definition; finish by checking both candidates.","config":{"steps":[{"id":"1","text":"Subtract x and factor: x(x − 1) = 0."},{"id":"2","text":"Over the real numbers, a product is zero only if at least one factor is zero, so x = 0 or x = 1."},{"id":"3","text":"Substitution confirms F(0) = 0 and F(1) = 1, so both candidates work and there are no others."},{"id":"0","text":"A fixed point x must satisfy x² = x."}],"correct":["0","1","2","3"]},"feedback":{"pass":"The argument finds every candidate and checks it. This is a worked algebraic proof, not a claim of machine-checked verification.","fail":"Definition → algebra → zero-product reasoning → substitution. A few plotted points would not establish exhaustiveness."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:fixed-point@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:nonlinearity-counterexample","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:nonlinearity@1","type":"number","stage":"predict","title":"Test superposition.","prompt":"Let f(x) = x² on ℝ. What is f(1 + 1) − [f(1) + f(1)]?","hint":"Compare 2² with 1² + 1².","config":{"value":2,"tolerance":0,"display":[]},"feedback":{"pass":"4 − 2 = 2. One failed instance is enough to refute the universal superposition requirement for this map.","fail":"Square after adding in the first term, but square before adding in the second."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:nonlinearity@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:nonlinearity-scope","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:nonlinearity@1","type":"choice","stage":"transfer","title":"Keep the claim small enough.","prompt":"What has the previous calculation established?","hint":"A counterexample refutes the particular universal property it targets.","config":{"options":[{"id":"0","text":"Every nonlinear map is chaotic."},{"id":"1","text":"The map x ↦ x² is nonlinear on ℝ; nothing about chaos follows from that fact alone."},{"id":"2","text":"Every recurrence is nonlinear."}],"correct":"1"},"feedback":{"pass":"Nonlinearity is a structural property. It is not a certificate of complexity or chaos.","fail":"The calculation concerns superposition for x², not the long-run behavior of every nonlinear system."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:nonlinearity@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:parity-sort","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:parity@1","type":"choice","stage":"predict","title":"Two kinds of integer.","prompt":"Which integer is odd?","hint":"An odd integer has the form 2k + 1 for an integer k.","config":{"options":[{"id":"0","text":"18"},{"id":"1","text":"19"},{"id":"2","text":"20"}],"correct":"1"},"feedback":{"pass":"19 = 2 × 9 + 1. The classification can drive a piecewise update rule.","fail":"19 leaves one after taking out pairs; 18 and 20 are even."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:parity@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:parity-witness","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:parity@1","type":"number","stage":"transfer","title":"Supply the witness.","prompt":"For 27 = 2k + 1, what integer k witnesses that 27 is odd?","hint":"Subtract one, then divide by two.","config":{"value":13,"tolerance":0,"display":[]},"feedback":{"pass":"27 = 2 × 13 + 1. The definition becomes a checkable arithmetic witness.","fail":"Solve 2k = 26, not 27."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:parity@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:periodicity-block","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:periodicity@1","type":"number","stage":"predict","title":"A declared repeating block.","prompt":"The infinite sequence is defined by repeating the block [1, 4, 2] forever. What is its least positive period?","hint":"The rule states that the entire block repeats. Its three entries are distinct.","config":{"value":3,"tolerance":0,"display":["1","4","2","1","4","2","…"]},"feedback":{"pass":"The least positive period is 3. This conclusion uses the declared infinite repetition, not just a finite prefix.","fail":"One or two steps does not return the first entry to 1. Three steps repeats the whole block."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:periodicity@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:periodicity-prefix","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:periodicity@1","type":"choice","stage":"transfer","title":"When does the cycle begin?","prompt":"A Collatz orbit beginning at 6 reaches the repeating cycle 1 → 4 → 2 → 1. What is justified about that orbit?","hint":"Distinguish the initial transient from a cycle reached under a known deterministic map.","config":{"options":[{"id":"0","text":"It is periodic from the initial value 6."},{"id":"1","text":"It is eventually periodic; the transient prefix beginning at 6 need not repeat."},{"id":"2","text":"Any finite repeated pattern proves the entire future of any unknown process."}],"correct":"1"},"feedback":{"pass":"Eventually periodic and periodic from the beginning are distinct statements.","fail":"The initial value 6 is not in the cycle. A transient can precede the repeating part."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:periodicity@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:recurrence-build","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:recurrence@1","type":"experiment","stage":"experiment","title":"One rule, different beginnings.","prompt":"Run the affine recurrence twice with different initial values. Then interpret what changed.","hint":"Change x₀ between the two runs. Keep the same multiplier and offset.","config":{"kernel_ref":"qeva:kernel:affine@1","parameters":{"a":2,"b":0,"x0":1,"steps":8},"editable":["x0"],"minimum_runs":2,"vary":"x0","conclusions":[{"id":"0","text":"The recurrence alone selected a unique orbit, regardless of its initial value."},{"id":"1","text":"The same update rule can produce different sequences when initial values differ."},{"id":"2","text":"Two different orbits disprove the recurrence."}],"correct":"1"},"feedback":{"pass":"Initial data is part of the experiment, not an optional footnote.","fail":"Compare the initial states. The rule is the same; its orbit also depends on the starting value."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:recurrence@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:recurrence-step","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:recurrence@1","type":"number","stage":"predict","title":"Use the machine.","prompt":"The rule is x[n+1] = 3x[n] + 1 and x[0] = 2. What is x[1]?","hint":"Substitute the present state 2 into the rule.","config":{"value":7,"tolerance":0,"display":[]},"feedback":{"pass":"3 × 2 + 1 = 7. The current state and the update rule determine this next state.","fail":"Apply the entire rule to 2: multiply by 3, then add 1."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:recurrence@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:recurrence-unique","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:recurrence@1","type":"choice","stage":"transfer","title":"What is missing?","prompt":"Someone gives only a[n+1] = 2a[n]. What additional information selects one sequence in this setting?","hint":"Compare the sequences beginning at 1 and at 3.","config":{"options":[{"id":"0","text":"A starting value, such as a[0] = 1."},{"id":"1","text":"A graph that looks smooth."},{"id":"2","text":"A sufficiently large number of likes."}],"correct":"0"},"feedback":{"pass":"With an initial value and this single-valued update, each later value is determined. Not every general recurrence guarantees uniqueness.","fail":"For this recurrence, the starting value is the missing information."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:recurrence@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:sensitivity-compare","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:sensitivity@1","type":"experiment","stage":"experiment","title":"A tiny difference. The same rule.","prompt":"Run the paired logistic recurrence. Change r and run again. Observe how the separation changes before choosing an interpretation.","hint":"Compare r = 2.8 with r = 3.9. Read the exact definition below before making a universal claim.","config":{"kernel_ref":"qeva:kernel:logistic@1","parameters":{"r":3.9,"x0":0.4,"delta":0.0001,"steps":70},"editable":["r","x0","delta","steps"],"minimum_runs":2,"vary":"r","conclusions":[{"id":"0","text":"A separated pair proves the formal sensitivity condition for every point in the state space."},{"id":"1","text":"These runs record finite numerical separation for declared parameters; they do not establish all quantifiers in the definition."},{"id":"2","text":"Every small perturbation must grow at every step."}],"correct":"1"},"feedback":{"pass":"You have reproducible observations. The universal sensitivity claim still requires a specified space, metric, and proof.","fail":"Two trajectories test particular starts and times. The definition quantifies over every base point and every neighborhood."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:sensitivity@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:sensitivity-quantifiers","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:sensitivity@1","type":"classification","stage":"verify","title":"Which quantifier is doing the work?","prompt":"Match each role in the archive’s metric definition of sensitivity.","hint":"The order is: exists δ; for every x and ε; exists y and n.","config":{"labels":[{"id":"exists","text":"There exists"},{"id":"every","text":"For every"}],"items":[{"id":"0","text":"A positive separation threshold δ, chosen once for the system.","label":"exists"},{"id":"1","text":"A base point x and a positive neighborhood radius ε.","label":"every"},{"id":"2","text":"A nearby point y and a time n, allowed to depend on x and ε.","label":"exists"}]},"feedback":{"pass":"The quantifier order is essential. One impressive-looking trajectory cannot discharge it.","fail":"δ is uniform. The point x and radius ε are arbitrary. The witness y and time n may depend on them."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:sensitivity@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:sequence-index","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:sequence@1","type":"choice","stage":"transfer","title":"Name a position, not a pile.","prompt":"For the rule aₙ = 2ⁿ with indexing beginning at n = 0, which statement is correct?","hint":"Write the indices 0, 1, 2, 3 underneath 1, 2, 4, 8.","config":{"options":[{"id":"0","text":"a₃ = 8, because the fourth displayed term has index 3."},{"id":"1","text":"a₃ = 4, because every sequence must begin at index 1."},{"id":"2","text":"The index is irrelevant; only the set of values matters."}],"correct":"0"},"feedback":{"pass":"An index selects a value. Changing the indexing convention changes what a₃ refers to.","fail":"The indexing convention was declared as starting at zero. Match positions to values."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:sequence@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:sequence-next","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:sequence@1","type":"number","stage":"predict","title":"Make the next move.","prompt":"Start at 1. The declared rule is to double the previous value. What comes after 8?","hint":"Use the stated doubling rule, not a guess about an unexplained finite list.","config":{"value":16,"tolerance":0,"display":["1","2","4","8","?"]},"feedback":{"pass":"16. A rule turns your prediction into something another person can check. You have begun tracing a sequence.","fail":"Try doubling 8. A finite list by itself does not determine a unique continuation."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:sequence@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:stability-compare","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:stability@1","type":"experiment","stage":"experiment","title":"Perturb a fixed point.","prompt":"Run x[n+1] = a x[n] with x₀ = 0.01 for a = 0.5, then change a and run again. Compare contraction with amplification.","hint":"Try a = 2 on the second run. The fixed point 0 remains fixed in both models.","config":{"kernel_ref":"qeva:kernel:affine@1","parameters":{"a":0.5,"b":0,"x0":0.01,"steps":12},"editable":["a"],"minimum_runs":2,"vary":"a","conclusions":[{"id":"0","text":"A fixed point being unchanged guarantees that nearby states move toward it."},{"id":"1","text":"Nearby behavior depends on the multiplier; being a fixed point alone is not enough."},{"id":"2","text":"Any nonzero perturbation changes the mathematical rule."}],"correct":"1"},"feedback":{"pass":"A fixed point and its neighborhood answer different questions. The finite plots motivate, but do not replace, an all-time stability argument.","fail":"Both rules fix zero. Compare what happens to the nearby state rather than to zero itself."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:stability@1"]},{"schema_version":"qeva-activity/1","id":"qeva:activity:stability-proof","revision":1,"concept_ref":"qeva:learning:stability@1","type":"choice","stage":"transfer","title":"From finite observation to all time.","prompt":"For F(x) = x/2 on ℝ and fixed point 0, which argument proves Lyapunov stability?","hint":"The definition needs every sufficiently close start and every nonnegative time.","config":{"options":[{"id":"0","text":"Twelve plotted points stayed nearby, so every future point must do so."},{"id":"1","text":"For any ε > 0 choose δ = ε. If |x₀| \u003c δ, then |Fⁿ(x₀)| = |x₀|/2ⁿ ≤ |x₀| \u003c ε for every n ≥ 0."},{"id":"2","text":"Zero never moves, so every map that fixes zero is stable."}],"correct":"1"},"feedback":{"pass":"The bound covers every n ≥ 0, not only the displayed sample. Its assumptions and quantifiers are explicit.","fail":"A proof must cover all times and a neighborhood of starting states, not only the reference orbit."},"source_refs":["qeva:1:stability@1"]}],"routes":[{"schema_version":"qeva-learning-route/1","id":"qeva:route:evidence-and-proof","revision":1,"title":"A simple rule. An unfinished question.","goal_ref":"qeva:learning:collatz-conjecture@1","description":"Follow integer recurrence into cycles, finite evidence, and the difference between a successful run and a universal proof.","ordering":"prerequisite-topological","authority":"editorial-guidance-not-logical-dependence"},{"schema_version":"qeva-learning-route/1","id":"qeva:route:toward-chaos","revision":1,"title":"When a small difference grows","goal_ref":"qeva:learning:chaos@1","description":"Begin with a prediction. Follow recurrence, fixed points, stability and nonlinearity into the precise limits of a chaos claim.","ordering":"prerequisite-topological","authority":"editorial-guidance-not-logical-dependence"}],"source_digest":"12c96bd2392f4be04d5fb17bc67f165d193755024956be6778ca67a1dec1e51e"};
